Acid mine drainage (AMD) is generated during the extraction of coal and metal ores when pyrite (FeS 2 ), commonly found around ore bodies, is exposed to air, subsequently reacting with rainwater and groundwater, and further oxidized by oxygen and water under microbial activity. The resulting acidic effluents have high concentrations of metals such as iron, manganese, arsenic, and aluminum as well as sulfate (Eq. ( 1 )). These contaminants pose serious environmental threats to mining regions. In the Umaria coalfield of India, concentrations of Fe and Mn in groundwater exceeded the permissible limits for drinking water in 40 and 14% of the samples, respectively, and the health risk index for children surpassed 1.0 in 76% of the samples, indicating a significant non-carcinogenic health risk [ 1 ]. In South Africa, AMD from the Witbank and Waterberg coalfields exhibited extremely low pH values ranging from 1.8 to 3.2 and was enriched in elements such as As, Pb, Mn, and Cu, posing moderate to high environmental risks [ 2 ]. These heavy metals may accumulate through the food chain, causing DNA damage, disrupt epigenetic regulation, and impair mitochondrial function, thereby posing serious threats to human health [ 3 ,  4 ,  5 ,  6 – 7 ]. In extremely acidic environments, biodiversity declines markedly; although diatoms such as  Pinnularia  and  Eunotia  can survive under elevated Fe, Zn, and Cu concentrations, the ecosystem structure undergoes profound disruption [ 8 ]. In the karst regions of southwestern China, AMD discharge facilitates the migration and accumulation of heavy metals in river sediments, destabilizing water chemistry and increasing ecological risks [ 9 ]. In summary, AMD contamination has emerged as a global threat to ecosystem integrity and public health, especially in mining regions of developing countries. 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
